And what gets expressed can change each time you open the same sketch. Take “The call is coming from inside the refrigerator.” Turn up the timing and you get comedy. Turn up the isolation and you get horror. Turn up the question of who pays attention to whom, and you get a little fable about the milk watching the family.

The epigenetics analogy fits the selective expression part. For Pantheon, there’s a second move: you can keep the sketch and also save what a particular reading discovered. The comedy version gives you “The milk is on line two.” Recompress that with one detail from the horror version and you get:

“The milk is on line two.”
“Then who’s on line one?”
“That’s what the milk wants to know.”

Now that becomes a seed. A future reader can open it as a joke, a mystery, or a story about two callers who each think the other is human. The pleasure is seeing which detail, once carried forward, changes what the whole thing can become.